at the end of the last session Steve we  were talking a little about the way in  which Isaiah encourages a continuous and  over time a long interpretation of the  original articles of Isaiah and Isaiah  himself seems to have been involved in  this kind of interpretation which sets  the pattern for later generations and it  continues certainly all the way down  into the New Testament period they're  reading this material and they're  entering into this great interpretive  tradition that was started by the  Prophet yeah and it continues all the  way into the modern period one if we had  time could track this long history of  the interpretation of this book which  seems to be what the writers of the book  originally intended for us to do so the  next section we're going to take up  begins to talk about Assyria and the  role of a foreign King in determining  Israel's destiny this is a really  unusual idea because it turns out that  God is personally involved in this kind  of activity so do you want to say a  little more about that where does this  idea of using the foreign King as an  agent of some sort where does all this  come from  in the Isaiah tradition well I'm glad  you mention the spot because the  meanings of God using all of these  different instruments or agents in this  book is absolutely fascinating and  there's quite a number as we push  through this series we're going to see  all sorts of figures and nations and  individuals being used as the tool or  instrument or agent of God and to just  try to understand what's going on is  it's quite interesting and to try to see  ourselves and in the role of some of  these agents is interesting certainly  the community of the second Isaiah  thought that it was well worth  considering how this nation of Assyria  which was this militaristic hubristic  violent superpower could possibly be  used by God in God's purposes and just  reading some of this material in  chapters 10 through 12 it really becomes  clear how how arrogant and how full of  themselves this speak this nation of  Assyria was it's one of the things that  second Isaiah is very clear about is  that because God in the view of Isaiah  is this lofty transcendent being of  other nests any human pretensions to  power and arrogance and hubris are just  ridiculous and because Assyria  encapsulate that they they come in for  judgment  so the question gets raised well how can  God both use this power as an instrument  but also judge it at the same time it's  it's really very very interesting the  second Isaiah has a comment on it in  verse 12 of chapter 10 amidst all this  poetry there's a little prose editorial  work and they talk about the work of the  Lord with Assyria being completed it's  the language of God having this plan  this long-range view because God is  transcendent because God is outside of  the cosmos because God is the creator  because God is totally other than what  we are God has this ability to see the  long-range widescreen drama of history  and these agents like Assyria have very  limited and circumscribed roles they  can't destroy God's people for example a  line in the sand is drawn before Zion  before Jerusalem verse 12 says it will  be when the Lord has completed all God's  work on Mount Zion then Assyria is going  to get their punishment so one thing  that emerges is okay the people of the  Lord may go through some very rough  times the water can come up to the neck  but God has a plan and that plan  involves never letting them go under and  never let  down certainly for people today a word  of comfort that God will never let God's  people be dragged under so that all  breath is lost yes it is a word of  comfort and but it Bray it raises  another traditional theme of the second  Isaiah and that is the notion of the  remnant the people who survive are the  remnant and that is a characteristic  notion in Isaiah that the people will  never be wiped out ultimately the idea  of the remnant seems to go back to the  idea of the promise to Jerusalem that it  will never be destroyed this arises in  2nd Samuel 7 where Nathan the Prophet  gives a Oracle of reassurance to David  and promises that his dynasty will be an  eternal dynasty and that God will choose  the City of Jerusalem in perpetuity and  eventually this was transformed in  Israelite thinking into the idea that  the city itself would never be captured  so there is an implicit promise to the  people that there will always be this  remnant in Jerusalem but the important  thing to notice about it is that the  remnant as Isaiah thinks about it does  not get off the punishment in order to  be a part of the remnant you need to go  through the punishment they are the  people who are sitting there with the  water of the Assyrian invasion rising  until it's up to their necks they're  barely able to keep their heads above  water the memorable image that already  is introduced in chapter 6 in the  initial call vision is that this is like  a forest which has been cut down the  remaining wood and the stumps have been  burned yeah but there will from the  stump come up this little sprig of new  life one gets the impression that  is really not much fun to be part of  Isaiah's remnant no I think that's  exactly right and when you get to this  beautiful passage about the stem of  Jesse that we all sing about in Advent  this coming of this messianic sprig  that's going to push itself up in  chapter 11  it really does presuppose this theology  of a remnant it's not like the tree is  growing strong and all of a sudden it  bears new fruit it's a stump the people  of God have been through some awful  trials they've been through an awful  torrent of flooding judgment but out of  that there's this tremendous new life  that's that's going to burst  interestingly enough it's not the  promises to David it's not the Davidic  royal line that's the subject of this  messianic Oracle in Chapter 11 they go  back to Jesse David's father as if to  say we're really going back to the roots  we're really almost digging underground  to find the holy seed way back beyond  everything that David and his children  ever did does that indicate that they're  already having some doubts about the  longevity of the Davidic line when this  is reinterpreted I think you have to  struggle with what what one imagines the  Messiah is if you imagine that the  Messiah is just an affirmation of human  race I just means the vindication of  David's claims to power and all that the  Kings and David's line did then yeah I  think they really need to rethink that  because the god of this material renders  all human claims and pretensions  ridiculous I think they're really trying  to get back when they're when they're  looking at this remnant to just a new  spirituality they're wrestling with that  and part of that new spirituality is  just a new way of being God's vicar on  earth this is a person who isn't judging  by what  his eyes see he is not working by the  normal patterns of power and  administration in the world but  something much more intuitive something  that cares much more about the poor  something that's willing to give the  self a backseat and to be censored on  others  careful concern for the neighbor concern  for the whole community even the weakest  among them it's a in some ways it is a a  critique of traditional royalty  traditional messianic thought it's a new  I think it's a new vision of what it  would mean to have a messiah and what it  would mean to have a remnant organized  around this Messiah so ultimately we can  look forward in the rest of the book  probably to ideas about how that  transformation is going to take place  and what the new Messiah the new royal  personage is going to be yeah I think we  should all keep our eyes open for  exactly that
 

